</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>
<span id="S4.T2.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dense Video Captioning and Event Localization results compared to SOTA</span>. VITT, YouCook2 and ActivityNet datasets. We use the standard evaluation metrics: SODA (S), CIDEr (C), METEOR (M), and localization (F1 score).
Our method does not use video pre-training, similar to the models at top. The models in the middle section use large pre-training (e.g. YT-Temporal-1B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al.(2023)Yang, Nagrani, Seo, Miech, Pont-Tuset, Laptev, Sivic,
and Schmid</a>]</cite>, HowTo100M <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al.(2024)Wu, Liu, Qiao, and Sun</a>]</cite>, WebLI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al.(2024)Zhou, Arnab, Buch, Yan, Myers, Xiong, Nagrani, and
Schmid</a>]</cite>).
The bottom section, including ours, are SSM-based approaches, all others use Transformers.
Vid2Seq is the version with visual-only inputs.
<math id="S4.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dagger" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>†</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dagger</annotation></semantics></math>: Methods which use datasets that are not public.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comparison to SOTA.</span> Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4 Experimental results ‣ Time-Scaling State-Space Models for Dense Video Captioning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows the main results of our (STS<span id="S4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\xspace</span>) approach compared to the state-of-the-art (SOTA).
Our model shows strong performance, outperforming most prior SOTA approaches, despite not using additional video pre-training.
We note that DIBS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al.(2024)Wu, Liu, Qiao, and Sun</a>]</cite> and SDC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al.(2024)Zhou, Arnab, Buch, Yan, Myers, Xiong, Nagrani, and
Schmid</a>]</cite> both use large and non-public video pre-training datasets.
Our model makes large gains in SODA and METEOR <span id="S4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1.5x)</span> for YouCook2 and ActivityNet and CIDEr and METEOR for VITT.
Our model outperforms the other SSM-based approach, VideoMambaSuite (VMS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al.(2024)Chen, Huang, Xu, Pei, Chen, Li, Wang, Li, Lu, and
Wang</a>]</cite> by large margins.
We compare to video-only inputs as this is adopted in most contemporary dense video captioning evaluation protocols, and because the additional ASR input, used in early works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al.(2023)Yang, Nagrani, Seo, Miech, Pont-Tuset, Laptev, Sivic,
and Schmid</a>]</cite>, artificially boosts performance, as it is often aligned with captions. Our vision-only setting is more challenging, relying on only video inputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Efficiency.</span> Our approach uses <span id="S4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">7x fewer FLOPs</span> in comparison to prior work (Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4 Experimental results ‣ Time-Scaling State-Space Models for Dense Video Captioning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), e.g., by our calculation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al.(2024)Zhou, Arnab, Buch, Yan, Myers, Xiong, Nagrani, and
Schmid</a>]</cite> uses 12800 GFLOPs for 128 output tokens. Our STS<span id="S4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\xspace</span>uses only 1854 GFLOPs, or 231 GFLOPs per segment, which can be linearly scaled to any video length.</p>
</div>
